<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lancashire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lancashire is a county in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that became the centre of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textile industry during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. Its mills processed raw cotton imported from overseas and exported finished cloth across the world, including to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The industry tied Lancashire closely to the trade networks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lancashire.jpg" alt="Lancashire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter McDermott · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clitheroe_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4067941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lancashire is a ceremonial county in North West England covering 3,079 square kilometres, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> to the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> to the west. The west contains flat coastal plains, including the Fylde, while the east is upland, with the West Pennine Moors in the south-east and the Forest of Bowland in the north-east. The rivers Lune, Wyre and Ribble flow west into the Irish Sea. The largest settlement is the city of Preston, and the county also holds Blackburn, Burnley, Lancaster and the seaside resort of Blackpool.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the area belonged to the territory of the Brigantes, and towns including Manchester, Lancaster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribchester">Ribchester</a> grew around Roman forts. After the Roman withdrawal in 410 CE the north probably formed part of the Brythonic kingdom of Rheged. In the mid-8th century the land north of the Ribble was incorporated into the Anglo-Saxon Kingdom of Northumbria and the land to the south into Mercia, both of which later became parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>The county was established in 1182 and was divided into the hundreds of Amounderness, Blackburn, Leyland, Lonsdale, Salford and West Derby. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 much of what became Lancashire had been treated as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. Until the early modern period it was a comparatively poor backwater, although in 1351 it became a palatine with a semi-independent judicial system. The Red Rose of Lancaster, the county's flower, recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lancaster">House of Lancaster</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>.</p><p>Lancashire was one of the homes of modern industrialisation, above all in textiles. It had a long history of supplying wool to weavers and had many cottager weavers by the 18th century, and increased imports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> needing processing triggered innovation. John Kay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-arkwright">Richard Arkwright</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-crompton">Samuel Crompton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hargreaves">James Hargreaves</a> were all from Lancashire. Until 1974 the county included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, a major port, and Manchester, which with its surrounding towns dominated textile manufacture. The Lancashire coalfield was also exploited, and canals including the Leeds and Liverpool Canal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal">Bridgewater Canal</a> formed part of the industrial network.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Manchester have lain outside the county. The Duchy of Lancaster, the private estate of the sovereign, still exercises the Crown's right in the County Palatine of Lancaster, whose boundaries remain the historic ones. Lancaster Castle, Lancaster Cathedral, Blackburn Cathedral and Hoghton Tower are among its notable buildings, and Lancashire hotpot, a lamb casserole, is a dish that originated there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lancashire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indian revenues paid for the Indian Army, the empire's largest standing force, while India was both a major market for <b>Lancashire</b> textiles and the keystone of Britain's position in the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome recruited Sarmatian horsemen, most famously the 5,500 Iazyges sent to Britain in 175 CE, and an inscription from Ribchester in <b>Lancashire</b> records a unit of Sarmatian cavalry still stationed there in the third century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotton textiles in <b>Lancashire</b>, coal, iron and engineering, and from 1830 the railways, beginning with the Liverpool and Manchester Railway, made Britain the ‘workshop of the world’, as displayed at the Great Exhibition of 1851.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1811 and 1816 the Luddites of Nottinghamshire, Yorkshire and <b>Lancashire</b> smashed machinery, and in 1812 Parliament made the breaking of stocking frames punishable by death.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Stockingers had taken organised action at various times since 1675, and new cotton-spinning technologies in <b>Lancashire</b>, which could be worked by less-skilled, low-wage labourers, met violent resistance in 1768 and 1779.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1652 he had a vision on Pendle Hill in <b>Lancashire</b>, in which he believed that &quot;the Lord let me see in what places he had a great people to be gathered&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Background</a></li></ul></section>
